.TH CLEAR_TERMINAL_HISTORY.PY 1 "2026-09-12" "2.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
clear_terminal_history.py \- inspect, audit and clear shell history, carefully
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B python3 clear_terminal_history.py
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B clear_terminal_history.py
(HistClean) finds the history files your shells and REPLs actually use, shows
what is in them, flags entries that look like leaked credentials, and removes
entries either wholesale or one pattern at a time — taking a compressed 0600
backup before it changes anything.
.PP
With no action flag it opens an interactive menu.
.PP
.B $HISTFILE
and
.B $ZDOTDIR
are honoured before the conventional paths. A tool that clears
.I ~/.zsh_history
while the shell writes somewhere else is worse than useless: it reports success
having deleted nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Actions
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the history files found, with entry counts, sizes, permissions and the
path each one was resolved from.
.TP
.B \-\-show
Show the most recent entries, one line each, with credential values masked.
See
.BR \-\-limit .
.TP
.B \-\-stats
Entry count, unique ratio, date span, credential count and the most-used
commands.
.TP
.B \-\-scan
Report entries that look like leaked credentials. Exits 3 if any are found.
.TP
.B \-\-dedupe
Remove duplicate commands, keeping the most recent occurrence of each.
.TP
.BI \-\-redact " PATTERN " [ PATTERN " ...]"
Remove entries containing any of these substrings, case-insensitively. With
.B \-\-regex
the patterns are treated as regular expressions instead.
.TP
.B \-\-redact\-secrets
Remove every entry
.B \-\-scan
would flag.
.TP
.B \-\-clear
Empty the selected history files. Confirmation requires typing
.B yes
in full.
.TP
.B \-\-fix\-perms
Set mode 0600 on any history file other users can read.
.TP
.B \-\-backups
List the backups taken before previous changes.
.TP
.BI \-\-restore " [ARCHIVE]"
Restore a backup, byte for byte. Prompts for which one when no archive is
named. The file being replaced is itself backed up first.
.SS Targets
.TP
.BI \-\-files " LABEL " [ LABEL " ...]"
Which histories to act on:
.BR zsh ", " bash ", " fish ", " python ", " node ", " sqlite ", " psql ,
.BR mysql ", " redis ", " irb ", " less ", or " all .
.TP
.B \-\-tools
Include REPL and client histories even for destructive actions.
.PP
With no
.BR \-\-files ,
read-only actions cover every history file that exists — a scanner that
silently skips
.I ~/.psql_history
is worse than useless — while actions that delete default to the shells alone
and say which tool histories they left out.
.SS Safety
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not prompt for confirmation. Required for any destructive action in a
non-interactive session; without it the tool exits 1 rather than proceeding
unasked.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Report what would change without writing anything.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-backup
Do not take a backup before changing a file. Without this flag, a change whose
backup fails is skipped rather than done blind.
.TP
.B \-\-shred
Overwrite the file's bytes with random data before clearing it. On APFS, or any
SSD, copy-on-write and wear levelling mean the old blocks may still exist; the
tool says so rather than promising an erase it cannot deliver.
.SS Output
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
How many entries or findings to display. Default is
.BR 20 .
.TP
.B \-\-regex
Treat
.B \-\-redact
patterns as regular expressions.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-secrets
Print matched credentials in full instead of masking them. Off by default: a
tool that prints your secrets in order to tell you they leaked has leaked them
again, into your scrollback.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " auto|always|never"
Force or suppress ANSI color. Default
.B auto
respects
.BR NO_COLOR ,
.BR TERM=dumb ,
and whether stdout is a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-ascii
ASCII-only output: no box-drawing characters and no emoji.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print only warnings and errors.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a machine-readable report to stdout; the human-readable UI moves to stderr
so stdout stays parseable.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success — nothing to do, or everything applied.
.TP
.B 1
Fatal error (unknown history label, a destructive action in a non-interactive
session without \-y, no backup to restore).
.TP
.B 2
Run completed but one or more files could not be changed.
.TP
.B 3
.B \-\-scan
found credential-shaped entries.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted with Ctrl-C or SIGTERM.
.SH FEATURES
.SS Removal Preserves Every Surviving Byte
Entries are parsed keeping the exact bytes they came from, and a rewrite emits
those bytes back verbatim. zsh extended timestamps, multi-line continuation
entries and non-UTF-8 bytes all survive a redaction untouched; only the entries
you asked to remove go.
.SS Credential Scanning
Seventeen rules cover AWS access and secret keys, GitHub, Slack, Google,
Anthropic and OpenAI tokens, JWTs, private-key headers, passwords embedded in
URLs, Authorization headers,
.BR "curl -u" ,
.BR "mysql -p" ,
.BR PGPASSWORD ,
.BR "ssh-keygen -N" ,
base64 payloads, and generic SOMETHING_TOKEN=value assignments. Values are
masked unless
.B \-\-show\-secrets
is given. Removing the entry does not un-leak the secret — rotate it.
.SS Backups Before Anything Destructive
Every destructive action first writes a gzipped copy to
.I ~/.histclean_backups/<label>_<timestamp>.gz
(mode 0600, in a 0700 directory), keeping the ten newest per file.
.SS The Shell That Undoes Your Work
A cleared file is refilled by any shell still holding that history in memory:
close the terminal and it writes its copy back over the top. After any content
change the tool says so and gives the per-shell incantation —
.B "HISTSIZE=0; HISTSIZE=10000"
for zsh,
.B "history -c"
for bash,
.B "history clear"
for fish — and notes that closing the other terminals first is the surest
route.
.SS Permission Auditing
History files are private by definition.
.B \-\-list
flags any that others can read;
.B \-\-fix\-perms
sets them to 0600.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
What exists, how big, and who can read it:
.EX
python3 clear_terminal_history.py --list
.EE
.TP
Audit every history on the machine for leaked credentials:
.EX
python3 clear_terminal_history.py --scan
.EE
.TP
Remove the entries it flagged:
.EX
python3 clear_terminal_history.py --redact-secrets
.EE
.TP
Forget every command mentioning a particular token:
.EX
python3 clear_terminal_history.py --redact 'ghp_abc123' --files zsh
.EE
.TP
Regex removal, previewed first:
.EX
python3 clear_terminal_history.py --redact 'export \\w+_TOKEN=' --regex --dry-run
.EE
.TP
Empty zsh history, overwriting the bytes first:
.EX
python3 clear_terminal_history.py --clear --files zsh --shred
.EE
.TP
Undo the last change:
.EX
python3 clear_terminal_history.py --backups
python3 clear_terminal_history.py --restore zsh_20260912_103843.gz
.EE
.TP
Feed an audit into something else:
.EX
python3 clear_terminal_history.py --json | jq '.files[] | select(.findings | length > 0)'
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.histclean.log
Timestamped log of every run. Mode 0600, rotated at 1 MB with 2 generations.
.TP
.I ~/.histclean_backups/
Gzipped backups taken before each change. Directory 0700, files 0600.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HISTFILE
Consulted first for the shell named by
.BR $SHELL .
.TP
.B ZDOTDIR
Consulted for the zsh history location.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Consulted for the fish history location.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, disables color under
.BR "\-\-color auto" .
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Python 3.10 or later, standard library only. Paths and defaults are tuned for
macOS.
.SH AUTHOR
Anirban Bagchi
